     <header><span className="alt-eyebrow">MATHEMATICS / EVIDENCE / LIMITATIONS</span><h2>What is actually calculated.</h2><p>Numerical verification is separate from experimental validation. None of the capstone methods has passed matched experimental validation.</p></header>
     <div className="science-columns"><section><h3>Implemented thermal proxy</h3><pre>{'Cp = mpeat cp,peat; Cw = mwater cp,water\nQeq = (Tp − Tw) / (1/Cp + 1/Cw)\nQ = Qeq [1 − exp(−UA (1/Cp + 1/Cw) Δt)]\nQdry = Cp (Tp − Tdry) [1 − exp(−UAdry Δt/Cp)]\nΔmdry = Qdry / Lsub ≤ remaining dry mass\nQwater = mwater cp,water ΔT + mevap Lv\nΔUpeat + Uwater + Hvapor,out + Qdry = 0'}</pre><p>Three independent 0.5 kg lumped specimens share a finite reservoir. Delivery and contact locations are prescribed; sample order is not a solved depth profile. Initial temperature 823.15 K, water inlet 293.15 K, fixed physical integration step 0.00625 s, default observation window 60 s. UA is assumed, not calibrated. No ambient heat loss, fuel reaction or oxygen transport is active in this proxy.</p><p>Incoming water carries sensible energy relative to the selected inlet reference. Vapor leaves at the assumed atmospheric boiling point, 373.15 K. Sensible exchange stops at the boiling boundary before latent transfer. Dry contact stops at the inlet-temperature validity boundary; no subambient ice physics is claimed. Source inventory, bypass, stored water heat and vapor export remain in separate ledgers.</p><p>Mass residual = initial supply − remaining − bypass − liquid retained − vapor exported. Energy residual = initial peat sensible energy − current peat energy − water sensible energy − vapor enthalpy − dry-source heat. Intervention off sets liquid/dry source inventories and thermal contacts to zero.</p></section>
     <section><h3>Preserved Peat Fire FEM</h3><pre>{'∂(θg ρg Yj)/∂t + ∇·(ρg q Yj − θg ρg D∇Yj) = Sj\nq = −K krg/μ (∇p − ρg g)\np = R T Σ(gj/Mj) / θg\n∂U/∂t + ∇·(ρg q cp,g (T−300)) − ∇·(k∇T)\n  = −Σ ΔHk rk + prescribed ignition\nrk = m0 10^logA exp(−E/RT) (m/m0)^n g(YO₂)'}</pre><p>Q1 hexahedral finite elements with lumped storage, stabilized conservative nodal transport, local five-step reaction and thermal/pressure Picard iteration. Two half steps are accepted after a full-step error estimate; failed candidates shrink the step. Maximum step is 1 s by default and integration is independent of render FPS. Top Robin heat/species exchange and prescribed atmospheric pressure; other faces sealed. Initial 300 K, 0.233 kg O₂/kg gas, 0.1 kg water/kg dry peat. Full equations, source terms, material assumptions and diagnostics remain in Peat Fire FEM → Physical formulas.</p><p>Energy residual includes ignition, reaction, outward heat and gas enthalpy. Total mass residual includes all five condensed inventories, four gas pools and boundary exchange. Suppression interventions remain disabled. CO₂ is never an oxygen source. No experimental validation is claimed for the transferred column kinetics in this cube.</p></section></div>
-    <h3>4.2.4 experimental field view</h3><pre>{'dm = min(remaining supply, prescribed flow × accepted Δt)\nHin = dm [4186 (Tin − 273.15) + pinterface / 1000]\ncorrected mass = raw mass residual − delivered mass\ncorrected energy = raw thermal residual − imported enthalpy'}</pre><p>The separate latest-render workspace owns a 1,280-cell reacting peat calculation, outside the comparison engines. It deposits finite liquid at an assumed advancing tip after each accepted step, resolves local equilibrium, and restores the whole candidate on failure. Interface pressure is assumed equal to local absolute gas pressure; pure-liquid admission requires inlet saturation pressure below that pressure. No pump power, liquid migration or vibration mechanics is solved. Its prepared hot initial field is independent of the original opening animation.</p><p>Calculated views show temperature, gas oxygen mole fraction and positive net liquid change in kg/cell, sampled at the middle-y slice and extruded for display. This liquid difference is not a source tracer. The default 60 s calculation remains hot and reacting. Site-view shaking, lateral jets, wetting and fire-out are authored illustrations and do not establish numerical extinction, suppression or a treatment ranking. Raw and source-corrected mass, species and energy ledgers are retained separately.</p>
+    <h3>4.2.4 vibration-only model status</h3><p>The requested method reuses the original fire-spreading and drilling scene, with authored vibration and dust cues. No supported vibration-force, mechanical-work, cyclic soil-response or transport-coupling calculation is available. Its predictive comparison execution is unavailable; shaking does not calculate cooling, oxygen loss, permeability change or suppression.</p><p>The earlier finite-liquid experimental field and its numerical evidence are preserved as historical work, outside the current vibration-only method. Its mass and enthalpy checks do not validate vibration. No liquid is delivered. The requested fracture, soil-settling and covered-fire ending is an authored concept illustration, not calculated compression, permeability change or extinction. Mechanical forcing, peat constitutive and damping data, and independent measurements are required before a predictive vibration response can be assessed.</p>
     <h3>Live comparison component capabilities</h3><div className="science-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Method / variant</th><th>Modeled comparison component</th><th>Missing physics</th></tr></thead><tbody>{VARIANTS.map(v => <tr key={v.id}><td>{v.number} {v.label}</td><td>{v.status.modeled}<br/>Experimentally validated: no</td><td>{v.limitation}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
     <h3>Material sources and validity</h3><p>SI internally; temperature in kelvin. Contact model does not contain concentration fractions. FEM oxygen is mass per total gas mass; FEM moisture is water per initial dry peat mass. Geometry, initial conditions, seed and observation window must match before a fair physical comparison.</p><ul>{MEDIA.map(m => <li key={m.id}><strong>{m.label}:</strong> {m.provenance}</li>)}</ul><p>Peat cp = 1840 J/(kg K) is the existing constant approximation. Water cp and latent heat are near-ambient/atmospheric-boiling constants; no broad temperature or pressure accuracy is asserted. CO₂ latent heat is 6030 cal/mol × 4.184 / 0.0440095 kg/mol, frozen near 195 K; it is not a general phase diagram. Unspecified pressure sources have no thermodynamic prediction.</p><p><a href="https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C124389&Mask=4" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NIST CO₂ phase-change data / Giauque & Egan (1937)</a> · <a href="https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C7732185&Mask=2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NIST water reference data</a> · <a href="https://doi.org/10.1071/WF16198" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Huang & Rein (2017), five-step peat kinetics</a> · <a href="https://doi.org/10.1071/WF20117" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Santoso et al. (2021), measured water-suppression context</a></p>
     <h3>Suppression and re-ignition assessment</h3><p>N/A for contact proxies and conceptual stages: cooling is not extinguishment. FEM can record reaction activity, oxygen, peat/char inventories and thermal history over its 600 s window; it does not model these capstone interventions. No success threshold, re-ignition guarantee, or winner is inferred from a lower temperature.</p>
